A||||4||stick shift||Car overheated, now it won't start||||8||
Z||000000||stick shift||12-14-2000||09:10 PM||||I would appreciate any information on this subject.  I purchased an 87 GT 2.8 liter 9 mo. ago and other than replacing bad water pump and other maintenance items, have had no problems until last week. On the way to work, the temp gauge started climbing until the temp light on dash came on.  I immediately shut off engine and coasted into parking lot.     That night the car started fine but after about 1 1/2 miles the temp gauge peged again so again I shut off engine and pushed it to  a service station .  I was low on coolant so after filling I thought my problems were over except for finding out where the coolant went(couldn't see any leaks). Car started fine but died before I got home.  After sitting a few minutes, started up and I drove home.  Next morning, started up as usual and I headed for work.  After  about  6 miles on highway I started to lose power and car finally quit altogether.  I havent been able to start it since.  Checked distributor first since I haven't replaced any parts there.  Pick up coil, cap, rotor and wires didn't look too good so I replaced them.  I am getting good spark.  Haven't replaced plugs but I pulled one and it looks pretty good but had gas on it so I assume fuel system is working.  Any suggestions for my next step?||||reg||8
Z||000001||Homicidal||12-15-2000||04:01 AM||||Check the ignition control module?<P>Just a suggestion.  Same happened to me, car stopped and wouldn't start again.<P>Check starter + starter cables?<P>Groundings?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||tgowens||12-15-2000||04:07 AM||||stick shift, just to let you know, when the temp rises like that going towards overheating, turning on the air conditioner will kick on the fan and cool the engine down........and, it also sounds like an ignition module to me. Also, check the temp guage wiring (small two prong plug to the left and above the #5 spark plug - these plugs usually heat and break from the manifold heat.......or, it could be a bad thermostat!||||reg||1
Z||000003||JetroGT||12-15-2000||07:38 AM||||Check your oil. Maybe the coolent found its way past a gasket.||||reg||1
Z||000004||stick shift||12-15-2000||05:29 PM||||Wow! I never had a blown head gasket or cracked head before so that was the last thing I was looking for. The stuff in my crankcase is about 4 inches over the fill mark and looks like pea soup. I hope I didn't ruin the engine.  I have a little time off from work and I hate to have anyone else work on my car so I,ll probably try to fix it. I gotta have my car back.  Thanks for your help.||||reg||1
